{
  "$comment": "Stands in for GET /api/v1/admin/users/{id}/memberships (the history), GET /api/v1/admin/memberships/{id} (one term) and the write endpoints under /memberships/{id}/*. ONE ROW PER TERM. A renewal is a NEW row carrying previousMembershipId — nothing here is ever overwritten, which is what makes renewal rate, plan transitions and lifetime value reconstructable at all (UM-BR-04, UM-AD-03). `cycle` and `entitlements[]` are the SNAPSHOT frozen at activation: the duration and price actually bought, and the plan's features as they read that day. THE CATALOGUE IS NOT OURS — plans and their billing cycles live in the Plans module and are read from `v1/admin/plans/` (UM-OD-01, resolved: consume, never define). A term therefore stores everything it needs to render and never looks a plan up, which is what makes it survive the plan being repriced, renamed or archived (UM-BR-12). `planId`/`planCode` are references back into that catalogue for traceability, not a dependency: the ids in this seed are placeholders and will be the server's own once the API lands. `events[]` is append-only and is the module's lifecycle audit — every pause, suspension, cancellation and reactivation carries its actor and its reason. `source` records what AUTHORISED the term; it holds a reference into Deals / Invoicing / Finance and never an amount, because this module writes no money (UM-BR-06). A term with source.kind = legacy has no reference and says so rather than being given an invented one.",
  "generatedAt": "2026-08-25T09:00:00+05:30",
  "pausePolicy": "continue",
  "$comment_pausePolicy": "UM-OD-04 IS OPEN. `continue` (the end date runs on while paused) is what this seed assumes, and every screen that depends on it names the assumption instead of presenting it as settled. The three candidates are continue | freeze | extend.",
